id: 1920
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 1920, "run_id": "run_a3995bac559c4563", "data": {"id": "9a1e4519-56de-4e2c-bf7f-789ed5d8ea7b", "run_id": "run_a3995bac559c4563", "kind": "text", "position": 1920, "revision": 1920, "parent_tool_use_id": "call_e7e057924f3a40a980c8d2d2", "agent_call_id": "b3ffe44d-6be1-4f63-8a26-cdae502c8404", "data": {"text": "Now I see the pattern. The existing (wrong) output had `1.32127` as the first F20 value, which matches `index_1=3, order=0` for 22.5eV. So in this dataset's naming convention, \"F20\" refers to `index_1=3` (the l=3 Legendre coefficient). This is because the data uses a different indexing: index_1=0 is the count/label, index_1=1 is l=1, index_1=2 is l=2, index_1=3 is l=3 (F30), etc.\n\nWait - let me reconsider. In standard notation, F_lm means Legendre coefficient of order l with azimuthal quantum number m. F20 would be l=2, m=0. But looking at the data, `index_1=2` gives very small values (~0.004) while `index_1=3` gives larger values (~1.3). Let me check what makes physical sense and look at the coordinate definitions.\n\n"}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:59:46.683396+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:59:46.683403+00:00"}}

